# [[.Name]]

A starter Goa / Goa-AI service created by `surla init` or the `init_project`
MCP tool, with a starter evaluator suite that `surla verify` and `verify_change`
can run against it.

## What is here

- `design/` declares the `[[.Name]]` service. `ask` (POST /ask) runs the
  `helper` agent on a question. `price` (GET /prices/{item}) returns an item's
  price in cents. The agent can call `price` through its `lookup_price` tool.
  `override_prompt` (PUT /prompts/{prompt_id}) replaces the helper's system
  prompt text until the process exits; it requires the key in the
  `SURLA_LIVE_TARGET_KEY` environment variable, which Surla sets for a live
  target, and rejects every call without one. `key` is left optional in
  `design/design.go` so a missing `X-Live-Target-Key` header reaches
  `Service.APIKeyAuth` and returns `401` (`unauthorized`) rather than `400`
  (`missing_field`) from the HTTP decoder; if you pass a security credential
  field to tools with `Inject(...)` (which requires the payload field to be in
  `Required(...)`), check for a missing header in HTTP middleware before the
  generated decoder runs when you want missing headers to answer `401`.
  `design/model.go` declares the C4 architecture model.
- `gen/` is generated from `design/`. After changing `design/`, call the
  `generate_code` MCP tool when connected to `surla serve` (or run `surla gen`
  in this directory): it runs Goa's generator for this module inside Surla's
  sandbox. Never edit `gen/`. Generation compiles the design only, so update the
  hand-written code in `internal/` and `cmd/` to match it, then build and test
  your uncommitted files with `check_project` as described in "Next steps".
  `AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md` is generated with it and maps the agent, its tools
  and the generated packages for coding agents. Its `goa gen`, `goa example`
  and `go run` commands run code on your machine outside Surla's sandbox, and
  this starter already includes what `goa example` would scaffold; in this
  project, follow "Next steps" instead.
- `internal/helper` is the agent's planner and `internal/service` implements the
  service. `Service.Ask` builds an in-memory runtime and store per question
  because this starter has no multi-turn sessions or `Confirmation` tools, and
  `Service.Price` returns expected outcomes as a result value (`known: false`)
  because errors returned by bound service methods that do not implement
  `planner.ToolFailureProvider` end the agent run. If you add a tool with
  `Confirmation` (which pauses the run before the bound method's own
  authorization runs; the host can also deny unauthorized calls immediately
  when handling the suspension), build one runtime and store in `main` so
  paused runs can be resumed, as Surla's `examples/orders` reference service
  does.
- `cmd/[[.Name]]` serves the API. The agent runs in memory and sends every
  model call to a goa-ai model gateway given with `-model-gateway host:port`;
  the gateway holds the model provider and its API keys.
- `eval/` is a separate Go module: the starter evaluator suite. It runs the
  `[[.Name]]` binary in Surla's sandbox and serves a scripted model (not an
  LLM) through its own model gateway. Its three scenarios are:
  - `prices_tea`: asks "How much is tea?" and checks that the agent called
    `lookup_price` and answered from that call's result;
  - `unknown_item` (tagged `holdout`): asks "How much is coffee?", which the
    catalog does not price, and checks that the agent calls `lookup_price`
    and reports that it found no price;
  - `prompt_override_reaches_model`: checks that `override_prompt` rejects a
    missing or wrong key and that the next question's model request carries the
    new prompt text. Its `--live` mode starts the
  `[[.Name]]` for `surla serve`'s `start_live_target`, planning with the model
  `surla serve` was started with. After changing
  `eval/design/`, regenerate its code with
  `surla gen -dir eval` (or call `generate_code` with `target: "eval"`).

The evaluator suite lives in this repository, so anyone who can read the code
can read its scenarios. Its `holdout` tag only hides that scenario's details
in the report view `surla verify -builder-view` prints; it is not a secret
test. Keep real holdout scenarios in an evaluator repository builders cannot
read.

## Next steps

If you created this project over MCP with `init_project`, `surla serve` has
already initialized the Git repository, created the initial commit (`commit` in
`init_project`'s result), bound the server to this directory (`dir`), and
pinned the evaluator suite to that initial commit. If you created it from the
CLI, first make the initial commit, then start `surla serve` and register it
with your coding agent as described in "Connect a coding agent" below.

For each change, with `surla serve` running on this directory:

1. Describe the change with `propose_change` and `confirm_intent`, and
   preview its architecture with `preview_architecture`.
2. Edit the code. When you edit `design/`, first call `generate_code` (with
   `target: "eval"` when you edit `eval/design/`), then update the hand-written
   code to match.
3. Call `check_project` to build and test your uncommitted files, this module
   and `eval/`, in Linux containers (Docker required). When it cannot download
   dependencies and its output shows a missing `go.sum` or `go.work.sum` entry
   or a `go.mod` update, call `update_dependencies` and check again.
   `read_check_output` returns the captured output, page by page, for the
   `op_id` in either result.
4. Commit, then pass the new commit ID as `candidate_commit` to
   `start_live_target` and `verify_change`.

See the
[MCP tools reference](https://github.com/goadesign/surla/blob/main/docs/REFERENCE.md#mcp-tools)
for each tool's inputs and results.

If you created this project from the CLI with `surla init`, initialize the Git
repository, make the first commit, and run `surla preview` and `surla verify`:

```sh
git init
git add .gitignore README.md AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md go.mod go.sum design gen internal cmd eval
git commit -m "Start [[.Name]] from surla init"
surla preview -dir .
VERIFY_DIR="$(mktemp -d)"
WORK_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/work"
REPORT_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/reports"
surla verify \
  -candidate-repo . -candidate-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -binary [[.Name]]=./cmd/[[.Name]] \
  -suite-repo . -suite-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -suite-module eval -suite-command ./cmd/[[.Name]]-evals \
  -request-revision starter -work-dir "$WORK_DIR" -report-dir "$REPORT_DIR" -builder-view
```

`surla verify` keeps `$WORK_DIR` after the run: it holds the clones, the
built binaries, and the evaluator's private evidence, including each build's
log and each step's output in `$WORK_DIR/evidence/`. `-report-dir "$REPORT_DIR"`
always writes the full verification report outside the repository as
`$REPORT_DIR/<digest>.json` (used by `surla approve`), while `-builder-view` hides holdout scenario names and checks
in the completed report JSON and summary line (if a run fails before writing a
report, `stderr` prints operator setup/execution failure diagnostics). Do not
share `$WORK_DIR`, `$REPORT_DIR`, or setup/execution failure output with coding
agents that build the service.

`surla verify` checks a commit. The CLI has no command that builds and tests
uncommitted files, so for later changes, start `surla serve` in this directory
and connect your coding agent (see "Connect a coding agent" below), and run the
`check_project` loop in "Next steps" above before you commit.

## Connect a coding agent and try a live target

If `surla serve` is not already running (for example, after `surla init` and the
first commit above), start `surla serve` from this directory with
`-project .`, a bearer token of your choice, and a model. `-project` needs Git
2.45 or newer. `-project .` reads
this starter layout from the current `HEAD` commit, pins the evaluator suite
to that commit for `start_live_target` and `verify_change`, and stores Change
Cards, verification runs, full reports, and live targets in operator-private
storage outside the repository (when started directly on a committed CLI
project, restart `surla serve` to trust a later `HEAD` suite commit; when the
project was created by `init_project`, restarting the original server restores
the initial suite commit saved in `workspace.json` unless you start `surla serve`
directly on the child project directory or pass `-verification-config`).
`-model-provider anthropic` reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
`-model-provider openai` reads `OPENAI_API_KEY`, and `-model-gateway-addr`
uses a goa-ai model gateway instead. Set `MODEL_NAME` to a model your provider
offers:

```sh
export SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN="choose-a-secret"
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="your-anthropic-key"
MODEL_NAME="your-model-name"
surla serve -principal you@example.com \
  -model-provider anthropic -model-name "$MODEL_NAME" \
  -project .

# Or with OpenAI:
export OPENAI_API_KEY="your-openai-key"
MODEL_NAME="your-model-name"
surla serve -principal you@example.com \
  -model-provider openai -model-name "$MODEL_NAME" \
  -project .
```

When `surla serve` is started from a standalone starter repository root
(`-project .`), you can pin a specific suite commit without following `HEAD` on
restart, or use a separate evaluator repository, by writing
`../[[.Name]]-ops/verification.json` outside the bound workspace and its
enclosing Git working tree and passing
`-project . -verification-config ../[[.Name]]-ops/verification.json` (if this
project is a subdirectory inside an enclosing monorepo or a child directory
inside a parent workspace bound before `init_project`, place the `<ops>`
directory outside the entire bound workspace and enclosing Git working tree
root, set `candidate_repo` and `suite_repo` to the Git repository root, and set
`candidate_module` and `suite_module` to the repository-relative module paths,
such as `"services/[[.Name]]"` and `"services/[[.Name]]/eval"`):

```sh
mkdir -p ../[[.Name]]-ops
cat > ../[[.Name]]-ops/verification.json <<EOF
{
  "runs_dir": "../[[.Name]]-ops/runs",
  "report_dir": "../[[.Name]]-ops/reports",
  "candidate_repo": ".",
  "candidate_module": ".",
  "binaries": {"[[.Name]]": "./cmd/[[.Name]]"},
  "suite_repo": ".",
  "suite_commit": "$(git rev-parse HEAD)",
  "suite_module": "eval",
  "suite_command": "./cmd/[[.Name]]-evals",
  "timeout": "30m"
}
EOF
```

Register the server with your coding agent. Connected coding agents can also
read Surla's Golden Path architecture and evaluator guide at the MCP resource
`surla://golden-path` (or `GET http://127.0.0.1:8090/golden-path`). For Claude
Code, save this as
`.mcp.json` in this directory. Claude Code fills in `${SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN}`
from its environment, so start it from a shell where that variable is
exported. `.mcp.json` holds that reference, not the token itself, and
`.gitignore` keeps it out of the repository:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "surla": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8090/mcp",
      "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer ${SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN}"}
    }
  }
}
```

Other coding agents take the same URL and header in their own configuration
files.

Then follow "Next steps" for each change. When `surla serve` is bound to this
workspace (`-project .`), `propose_change` reads `design/*.go` from the
workspace automatically when `design_source` is omitted (or pass
`design_source` explicitly when targeting a different design). The confirmed
card's `request_revision` is the value to verify and approve the change with.

To try prompt wording on a running `[[.Name]]` before you verify it,
call `start_live_target` with the full commit ID of the candidate you want to
run (the initial `commit` returned by `init_project`, or `git rev-parse HEAD`
after committing your changes), then `draft_override` with
`prompt_id` `[[.Name]].helper.system`. A draft lasts only as long as that
target: to keep it, write the text into `internal/helper/planner.go`, run
`check_project`, commit, and pass that new commit ID to `verify_change` (or
`surla verify`).

`-request-revision` names the confirmed change request the report is for.
`starter` is only a local label; when a change goes through the Surla server,
use the `request_revision` its Change Card shows, so `surla approve` and
`approve_merge` can match the report to that request.
